    <text>
        <front>
            <div type="summary">
                <p>
                    Barbara Hepworth writes regarding a review of Three Guineas from the Times Literary Supplement and brings attention to the book.
                </p>
            </div>
        </front>
        <body>
            <div type="letter">
                <opener>
                    <dateline>
                        <date when="1938-06-30">
                            30 June 1938
                        </date>
                    </dateline>
                   
				   <address>
						<addrLine>
							<persName>Dr. Violet Kelynack</persName>
						</addrLine>
                        <addrLine>
                            <orgName>Medicinal Women's Federation</orgName>
                        </addrLine>
                        <addrLine>
							9, Clifford Street
						</addrLine>
						<addrLine>
							Bond Street, W.1.
						</addrLine>
                   	 </address>
					 
                    <salute>
                        Dear Madam
                    </salute>
                </opener>
				
                <p>
				<persName>Dr. Violet Kelynack</persName>
				<lb/><choice><sic>Medcial Women's Federation</sic><corr>Medicinal Women's Federation</corr></choice>, 
				<lb/>9, Clifford Street, 
				<lb/>Bond Street, W.1. 					<add place="right"><date>June 30th, 1938</date></add>
				</p>
				
				<p>
				In reply to your card of <date>June 29th</date>, we 
				<lb/>are inclosing a review copy of <title>Three Guineas</title>. We shall
				<lb/>be interested to see your Quarterly Review with the
				<lb/>notice when it appears.
				</p>
				
				<p>
				As you will see, in <title>Three Guineas</title> <persName key="Q40909">Mrs. 
				<lb/>Woolf</persName> concerns herself with the present status of women, 
				<lb/>and with the urgent question <emph rend="underlined">What can a Woman do to Prevent
				<lb/>War?</emph> Like <title>A ROOM OF ONE'S OWN</title>, <title>Three Guineas</title> is essential 
				<lb/>reading for any <choice><sic>women</sic><corr>woman</corr></choice>with an interest in present day affairs, 
				<lb/>and who has not?
				</p>
				
				<p>
				We are having a number of cards printed
				<lb/>to bring this book to the attention of an even wider audience than
				<lb/>it has already obtained, and I am wondering whether you would be
				<lb/>willing wither to send it out to your members, possibly with 
				<lb/>material of your own, or if you would provideus with a list to 
				<lb/>whom we could dispatch.
				</p>
				
				<p>
				The book was the subject of a leader in a recent
				<lb/>issue of the <orgName type="Q50008">The Times Literary Supplement</orgName><note><p>The Times Literary Supplement is a renowed British literary and cultural review, founded in 1902</p></note>, in which <persName>Mrs. Woolf</persName> was 
				<lb/>escribed as the most brilliant pamphleteer in England, and it was
				<lb/>furthermore suggested that "this brilliant and searching pamphlet
				<lb/>might mark an epoch in the world's history." Let us hope that 
				<lb/>this may be so!
                </p>
				
                <closer>
                    <salute>
                        Yours very truly
                    </salute>
					<positionName>Manager</position>
					<add place="right"><signed>BH.</signed></add>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
